{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And they said, We will not go up.</b> To which mountain did he call them to go up? And what did they mean when they said, 'Shall the eyes of those men be put out'? The explanation of the matter is that Moses sent after them to speak to their hearts words of peace, but they suspected Moses, thinking that the reason he sent after them was because they are great men and the principal figures in this dispute, and Moses wishes to give them some appointment to authority by way of bribery, so that they would withdraw from the dispute, and then all the rest would withdraw automatically. Therefore they answered Moses: 'We will not go up' — we will not accept from him any position or authority by way of bribery. And concerning this they said: 'Shall the eyes of those men be put out' — for bribery blinds (Deuteronomy 16:19). Do you imagine you can blind our eyes with this bribery? And in order to justify the expression 'those men,' it appears that they did not say this about themselves but about all the men who were with them. For even if we were to remain silent because you give us some authority or an inheritance of field and vineyard, the men who are with us would all the more so become bolder — 'shall the eyes of those men' who are with us be put out, so that they would be so blind as not to sense this stratagem? Therefore, 'we will not go up' — we will not accept from you any authority or elevation." "And as for the fact that they inserted into this account 'Is it a small thing that you have brought us up' etc. — they were saying one thing and then more: First they said that such is your way — you promise and do not deliver. For so you promised the children of Israel to bring them into a land flowing with milk and honey, yet you brought us up from Egypt, a good land — and all this you did 'to lord it over us,' in order to display your authority over us. And not you alone, but 'you have also made others lord it over us,' such as Aaron and others. Not only have you not brought us to a land flowing with milk and honey, but you would give us an inheritance of field and vineyard, etc. — they said: whichever way you look at it, why do you send after us? If it is to promise us some inheritance of field and vineyard when we come to the land, this is a thing that has not come into the world at all, for you have not yet brought us there — will you give us now an inheritance of field and vineyard? And even if you say that our companions will not sense this stratagem, since this gift is for a distant time, there are nevertheless several flaws in it: first, that we shall not go up to the land at all, for a decree has already been issued that we shall die in the wilderness. And if you say that this gift will be for our children, what benefit is it to us? And for our children too the matter is doubtful, for it is your way to promise and not deliver. Furthermore, the thing has not yet come into the world at all. And if you wish to give us some authority immediately, there is yet another concern: our companions will sense this stratagem — will their eyes be put out so that they do not see this stratagem eye to eye?" "And there are those who say that this putting out of eyes refers to the hornet, for he promised them that HaShem would send the hornet before them (ibid. 7:20) in order that it would put out the eyes of the Canaanites. And they said: even if they should indeed be blind, the Canaanite is nonetheless stronger than we, and we cannot go up against the people. This is what they meant by 'we will not go up.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:43.710160547Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}